WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.110
JAY SMITH: Welcome
to CE Chat hosted

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:12.830
by Cloud OnAir, live
webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:16.250
Just a reminder, we are hosting
these webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:17.560
My name is Jay Smith.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.190
I'm a Cloud Customer
Engineer here at Google,

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:24.070
and today, I will be
talking to David Aronchick.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:25.460
Nice to meet you.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.050
DAVID ARONCHICK:
Thank you very much.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.841
JAY SMITH: Today, we're
going to be talking

00:00:28.841 --> 00:00:30.050
a little bit about Kubeflow.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:35.060
And just a reminder, guys, we
encourage you to ask questions

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.640
any time on the platform.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.449
And we have Googlers on
standby to answer them,

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:41.490
and we'll get to some of
them at the end as well.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.920
So let's get started.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.860
DAVID ARONCHICK: Great.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.000
Thanks so much, Jay.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.130
As you mentioned, we're
here to talk about Kubeflow.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.160
That's machine
learning on Kubernetes

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.580
or, as we like to
say, Cloud Native ML.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.010
And I think it's a
really interesting time

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.310
to be talking about machine
learning and Kubernetes

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:02.120
together.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.470
The reality is that everyone
hears about how great ML is.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.930
They want to adopt
it and, you know,

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.985
immediately make changes
to their business.

00:01:10.985 --> 00:01:12.860
And I think the problems
that a lot of people

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.010
have today is it can
be very complicated,

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.790
and there's a big distance
between the actual vision of ML

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:22.380
and getting it going
off the ground.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.620
A lot of people today,
you know, say ML is great.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:26.630
They hear about it.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:27.522
It's in the news.

00:01:27.522 --> 00:01:29.480
They hear it from their
colleagues and friends.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.760
They want to go and solve a
bunch of problems very quickly.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.080
JAY SMITH: It's definitely
a big buzz word,

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.150
and everybody's trying
to invest in it.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.470
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:39.330
And the reason is because
of things like this.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.350
This is something that
Google did specifically.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:45.080
This is Google engineers,
Google data scientists

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.340
who spend their entire life
getting great at data centers,

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.820
using renewable energy, using
the latest technologies.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:56.210
And then they go and they
attach an ML solution

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:59.180
to the problem to handle
the power and cooling

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.220
and management of
their data centers.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:04.400
And almost instantly,
they were able to save

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.229
40%, a huge amount of money,
by attaching machine learning

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.690
models to these frameworks.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.950
So they were able to make a
huge difference, already better

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.890
than the best in almost
any industry just

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.640
by using this machine
learning technology.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.640
And this is something
that a lot of industries

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:23.750
are really excited about.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.000
And they are like, well, geez.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.710
You know, I'd love to
get some of that as well.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:30.620
Unfortunately, machine
learning is very hard.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.710
It's not just because
I'm terrible at math.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:37.450
It is because this is the
reality of machine learning.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.730
On one side, you have
that magical AI goodness,

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.900
which is so easy to see, where
the benefits are so powerful.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.830
And on the other side, you see
where most folks are today.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:54.830
They, you know, have old
style legacy infrastructure

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.000
they need to integrate with.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:57.950
They have very large
data sets that they're

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:01.160
trying to manage and maintain
and make the most use of.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:04.940
And there's so much
distance between, you know,

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:06.740
where they are today
and the magical AI

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:07.891
goodness of the future.

00:03:07.891 --> 00:03:09.890
JAY SMITH: Well, that's
pretty extensive, there.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:13.580
Just-- not a magic wand
like a lot of us think.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.114
Just throw good-- throw
pictures and get a result.

00:03:16.114 --> 00:03:17.030
DAVID ARONCHICK: Yeah.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.070
No, no question.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:22.130
There really is no magic
wand, and the reason

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:24.710
is there is no magic wand
is because you basically

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:26.180
have this reality.

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.220
There are two
sides of the world.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:32.180
Side one is where you have
these do-it-yourself solutions,

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.910
where you set it up from
scratch, where you integrate

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:36.230
with legacy systems.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.630
And then, once you've
finally done all that work,

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.940
which can be weeks,
months, years

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:42.650
in order to do
that, you often have

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:45.380
to do it all over again
as you need to migrate,

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:47.690
as you change your
solution, as anything

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:49.760
in your system overall changes.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.240
The other side of the
coin is around hosted.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.680
And I should say, this is
not all hosted solutions,

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.450
but certainly this is the trend.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.910
In the first five
minutes, it looks great.

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:01.550
You're immediately able
to make progress, change

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.580
the way you do your business
and at least experiment

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.540
with the model.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.670
But then in the
next five years, you

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:11.060
face everything you did in
the do-it-yourself scenario.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:14.180
You have the same
customization issues,

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:16.940
you have the same legacy systems
that you need to integrate.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:18.540
Plus now you're locked in.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.040
You've used a framework
or a model that

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.790
only works on a single cloud,
and you're kind of out of luck.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.210
So the question is, haven't
we heard this story before?

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.150
And the reality is we have.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.640
Before I came to
work on Kubeflow,

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.910
I ended up I was working
on the Kubernetes platform.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:41.270
And Kubernetes, when it
first came out in 2015,

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:43.670
it was a very,
very similar story.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:47.900
The reality was that containers
and Kubernetes in 2015

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:51.230
and before was a highly
bespoke solution.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:54.650
You had to come up with
a lot of custom tooling,

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:57.700
frameworks, and so on in order
to get things up and running.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.380
And in order to wire
everything together,

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.090
it became an enormous
amount of work.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:04.760
You had to have entire
teams dedicated to that.

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:07.060
When Kubernetes
came along in 2015,

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:12.060
you now started to have
a standard set of APIs

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.300
and native ways to handle the
complexities of distributed

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.170
computing.

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:18.640
And that really
changed the game.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.590
People started to leverage
the Kubernetes native platform

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:26.790
to go the next level up.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:31.840
In Kubecon in Austin
2017 in last December,

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.840
we talked about some of the
ways that we really have

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.572
started to unlock that future.

00:05:37.572 --> 00:05:40.030
JAY SMITH: You guys were just
at Kubecon EU as well, right?

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:41.830
DAVID ARONCHICK: That's
absolutely right.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:44.440
And the way that we
were able to unlock

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:48.490
that future was with these
native extension points.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:50.410
You have all these
various components

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:53.920
that operate in layers
from all those run

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.319
times and plug-ins, moving
up the stack to the new API

00:05:57.319 --> 00:05:59.110
layer, application
layer, governance layer,

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:00.260
so on and so forth.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:02.410
But each of these
had very clean lines,

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.660
and they enabled
people to swap out

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:08.200
the things that made the most
sense for running Kubernetes.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.880
And what that
ultimately resulted in

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:14.020
is people being able to run
cloud native applications.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.370
Applications for
running containers

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:18.160
that used all the
stat things that

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.890
were provided to them
without any customization.

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:24.280
And so what we would like to
propose around machine learning

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.970
is Cloud Native ML,
a similar approach

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:31.900
to this system which adopts
the core components of what

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:34.150
makes containers and
Kubernetes so strong.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:38.630
That is composability,
portability, and scalability.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:41.860
So for each of those, I think
it's worth just a moment

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:45.280
to talk about how they
apply to machine learning.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.840
When you think
about composability,

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.590
first, you think about building
a model around your machine

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.250
learning solution.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:54.250
And that's certainly
something very interesting.

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:58.870
Whether or not it's TensorFlow,
Caffe, CNTK, MXNet, PyTorch,

00:06:58.870 --> 00:06:59.510
you name it.

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:01.718
There are many, many different
ways to build a model.

00:07:01.718 --> 00:07:03.200
People get very
excited about that.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.860
But the reality is when it comes
to building a machine learning

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:09.250
solution, it ends up being
much much more than that.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:12.160
You end up having all these
various components that you

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:14.200
need to integrate, and
each of those components

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.600
are often slightly
different based on the data

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:17.730
scientist working on it.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:20.380
JAY SMITH: That's
definitely not magic there.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.080
That is a lot of work
and a lot of knowledge

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:27.520
and a lot of skills needed to
make any of this work, right?

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:28.630
DAVID ARONCHICK: Yeah.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:31.300
And I think that's
exactly right.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:35.140
Data scientists have
skills in particular areas.

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:37.180
They want to use
the frameworks that

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.730
are familiar to them, the tools
that are familiar to them,

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:41.200
in each of these various stages.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.720
From data, to experimentation,
to production,

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:45.340
and ultimately rolling it out.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.830
And I think that
really speaks to how

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:49.900
you have to have these
things be composable.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.750
So if you have a data
scientist in one organization,

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:54.640
they can use standard
techniques and then

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.650
swap them for a different set
of techniques for different data

00:07:57.650 --> 00:07:58.900
scientists and different work.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:07:59.770
JAY SMITH: All right.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.120
DAVID ARONCHICK: So
that's kind of category 1.

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.430
We want them to be composable
and let people choose

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:04.900
what makes sense to them.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:06.910
Category 2 is
around portability.

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.700
So earlier, we talked
about all the steps

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:11.870
involved a machine
learning framework,

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:13.900
but even that is just the start.

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:17.350
Then, you start to talk about
all the elements in your stack.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:20.280
OS drivers, run times,
so on and so forth.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.150
It gets very, very complicated.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:25.330
And to set up even one
of these environments

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:26.680
can be very challenging.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:31.420
Yet, the average data scientist
often faces multiple of these.

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:34.390
They might start on their
laptop or on a private rig.

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:37.770
They might move to a training
solution, a training cluster,

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:40.630
and then finally into the
cloud for final production

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:42.940
and training at scale.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:45.580
And to have a
portable solution that

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:48.280
works in all of these
places would greatly

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.350
speed their ability
to work instead

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:53.440
of having to have them
set up a custom bespoke

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:55.870
solution for each one.

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:58.180
And then finally, we
get to scalability.

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:02.620
Scalability is often thought of
as just the number of machines,

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:05.530
but really in machine learning,
it's much more than that.

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:09.760
It's accelerators, things
like GPUs, TPUs, CPUs,

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:11.650
the speed of your
disk and networking,

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:14.800
whether or not it's a
fixed disk, SSD, so on.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.200
And then it's not
just physical assets,

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:18.970
but it's actually human beings.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:21.360
How do you scale people
with different skill sets?

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.090
Interns, SUI's, data
scientists, researchers, IT ops.

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:27.940
How do you scale various
teams if they're geolocated?

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:30.560
Even if they're sitting
right next to each other,

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.770
but they happen to
have different focuses.

00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:36.160
And ultimately, how do
you get more experiments?

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:38.840
How do you run them
simultaneously and so on?

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:40.630
So those are the
three things that we

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:42.610
think make up Cloud Native ML.

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:46.740
Composability, portability,
and scalability.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.320
And what we think
is a great solution

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.390
for that is containers
and Kubernetes.

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:54.210
That's really what
they were designed for.

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:58.230
Loosely coupling a number of
services together and making it

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:00.970
very production
ready and scalable.

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:04.920
However, if you want to use
machine learning on Kubernetes,

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.430
it can be very challenging.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.510
There are a lot of core
components that are involved.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.750
Containers, packaging, service
endpoints, persistent volumes.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:14.540
These are often
very new concepts

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:18.330
to people who are familiar
really with just VMs and disks.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:20.925
And even worse, if you get
into the data scientist realm

00:10:20.925 --> 00:10:23.300
where they really just want
to focus on their experiment.

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:23.900
JAY SMITH: Right.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.233
I remember when I first started
getting into containers.

00:10:26.233 --> 00:10:28.640
It was very different
than what I used to do,

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:30.520
and I actually started
using containers just

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:33.600
as kind of like makeshift
VMs, not using them

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:35.040
to their full extent.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:38.141
And, you know, first you
have to learn that aspect.

00:10:38.141 --> 00:10:40.140
Then, you have to now
learn the machine learning

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.000
or, as you said, data
scientists just care about data.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:45.540
DAVID ARONCHICK:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.180
So that's why we
introduced Kubeflow, a way

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:52.650
to set up a complicated but
composable, portable, machine

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:56.220
learning framework in a
way that takes advantage

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:59.140
of all the advantages in the
climate of machine learning.

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:01.050
We want to make it
easy for everyone

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:03.750
to develop, deploy,
and manage portable,

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:06.340
distributed ML on Kubernetes.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:09.540
And it's really important
that we mention that last term

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:12.120
about focusing on Kubernetes.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.810
Anywhere there is a Kubernetes
deployment, this runs great.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:18.990
So whether or not
that's Google or on-prem

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:22.830
or on VMware or on
other clouds, as long

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.350
as there is a Kubernetes
conformant cluster,

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.480
you can set up and run Kubeflow.

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:30.600
And I will show you how
that works in just a second.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:33.030
The way to think about
it is thinking back

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:35.490
to all those various
stacks we had earlier.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.740
You have Kubernetes
as this base layer.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:39.570
You can be sure that
it runs everywhere.

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:42.060
That's a core vision of
the Kubernetes project.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:45.900
Then, you describe your
overall complex deployment

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:48.277
using Kubeflow native tooling.

00:11:48.277 --> 00:11:50.110
And you're able to stamp
out Kubeflow in all

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:52.230
of these various locations.

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:56.700
And specifically,
as Jay mentioned,

00:11:56.700 --> 00:12:00.000
at KubeCon EU on
Friday, we announced

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.830
Kubeflow 0.1, a significant
new milestone for us

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.260
to get out the door and
start helping people really

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:09.270
start to use production
or excuse me,

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.400
use Kubeflow in
their enterprises

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.890
to solve problems today.

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:17.010
Today in the box, we
have a Jupyter notebook.

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:19.380
We have distributed
training in the box.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:22.300
We use multi framework
model serving.

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.510
And I should really
stress right now

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:26.550
that though flow is
the name, we really

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:29.520
think about that as machine
learning flow and not

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:30.090
TensorFlow.

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:34.200
We support Caffe, PyTorch, and
we have lots more frameworks

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:35.070
coming soon.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.710
Our goal is to really
support whatever our data

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:42.660
scientists needs using standard
packaging for customizing

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:44.720
it yourself.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:48.220
And so with that, I'd like to
show you just a very brief demo

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.810
about Kubeflow.

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:52.780
Let me just swap over here.

00:12:52.780 --> 00:12:57.640
You'll forgive me, this was
a live demo this morning,

00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:00.430
but just because we have
to run on this laptop,

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:05.470
we're going to use a recorded
video of the one I did earlier.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:09.760
So what you have here is
a standard Yelp analysis

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:11.620
or excuse me, Yelp
sentiment database.

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:16.890
This is public data,
and in the public data,

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:21.450
basically there's all
the various results

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.550
from Yelp content.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:27.780
And they have large
comment databases,

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:30.630
and you're able to see
through and page through all

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:32.100
the various information.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:35.309
In this case, you have a
variety of different things.

00:13:35.309 --> 00:13:36.600
And looking at this middle one.

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:38.760
It's a lot of
words, but really, I

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:41.670
think, as humans, we're able
to see that it works properly.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.470
A little on the pricey
end for baked goods,

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:46.110
but the baked goods were good.

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:48.460
Also, their coffee
products are great.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:51.660
These are a lot of words,
but clearly as humans, we're

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:52.760
able to see it.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:56.280
However, when you apply this
to a standard machine learning

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:59.730
framework or excuse
me, a standard solution

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:03.930
today, it may not look as,
you know, as obviously correct

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:05.730
for these folks.

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:07.890
You know, it's often
very, very hard

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:11.430
using a regular
expression analysis

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:13.610
to have a system go
and look at this.

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:14.940
So let's actually try.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:16.524
And we hit the
prediction sentiment,

00:14:16.524 --> 00:14:17.940
and it didn't come
back very well.

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:20.770
So that's not exactly
what we want to see.

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:23.880
I think it's actually kind of
funny when you think about it.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.010
What you might come up with
for a solution here is,

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:29.400
well, maybe I'll just do some
regular expression matching

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:31.050
against the underlying code.

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:33.050
And looking at this regular
expression matching,

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:35.299
you're looking at it and
you're like, well, all right.

00:14:35.299 --> 00:14:36.150
You know, oh geez.

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:38.726
Someone commented
out good and great.

00:14:38.726 --> 00:14:40.350
We're just going to
try and find things

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:42.460
where it says annoying,
terrible, and bad.

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:44.290
You know, not ideal.

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:47.460
It kind of works, but
you know, obviously, it

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:48.960
missed on some of
the things that we

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:51.460
were looking for earlier.

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:53.220
So what I'd like
to do is, I'd like

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:56.130
to use machine learning to
try and solve this problem.

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:59.470
The Google team, the Google
Brain team as we call them--

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:03.240
actually, I think that
is literally their name--

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:05.640
released an open source
machine learning framework

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:09.790
called Tensor2Tensor that
is very, very well designed

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:12.750
or excuse me, kind of
pre-built for handling

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:14.820
a lot of these language issues.

00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:17.610
And you can see here this
is an entire set of things.

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:22.080
It describes how to install
and run these various things.

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:24.180
Which is obviously
pretty complicated.

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:25.890
But you can see
some of the things

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:29.490
that it's really good at.

00:15:29.490 --> 00:15:32.250
It's really good at
things like image language

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:34.240
or image classification,
language modeling,

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:36.710
sentiment analysis, and so on.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:39.000
And so really, that
sentiment analysis

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:41.640
is what we're going to
try and set up here.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:43.990
That's just a demo script.

00:15:43.990 --> 00:15:45.830
Everything here is
really happening.

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:48.060
That's just a demo script
to kick everything off.

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:49.620
And what we're
going to do first is

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:54.180
we're going to set up a standard
machine learning framework

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:57.000
on your local cluster using
something called Minikube.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.720
Minikube is an application
that runs on your laptop,

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:03.750
and it creates a real life
Kubernetes cluster running

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:06.270
locally so that you have
as little difference

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.910
between your laptop
and ultimately

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:10.960
what you run into in production.

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:12.840
So what we're going
to do here is first,

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:14.370
we're going to start
using Minikube.

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:16.350
Minikube is already
running in the background.

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:19.380
I'm going to switch
my Kube control

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:21.780
to point at that
Minikube, and then I'm

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:25.470
going to run a deployment
of Kubeflow Core.

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:29.790
That is a standard package
that we have in Kubeflow.

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:32.580
You just execute
literally that command,

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:34.650
that KS there
stands for Ksonnet.

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:37.620
And you apply it to
Minikube, and what happens

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:40.230
immediately is it
goes and deploys

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.420
all of the various
components that you

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:46.230
might want for a core
machine learning framework.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:47.670
It gives you TensorFlow.

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:49.020
It gives you JupyterHub.

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:52.230
It gives you an
ambassador for HTTP proxy.

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:54.810
It gives you a
distributed job framework,

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:56.310
and it gives you a dashboard.

00:16:56.310 --> 00:16:59.220
So really, really powerful
right out of the box.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:01.440
It gets going.

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:04.500
And what, like I said, it starts
up with a Jupyter notebook.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:07.057
In this case, it accepts
any user name and password

00:17:07.057 --> 00:17:08.890
so you don't want to
enable this to the web.

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:10.650
And then you get
it going, but you

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:13.140
can see here you're
able to go and select

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:16.170
an entire array
of various tools.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:19.050
We come with a number of
different packages built in

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:22.170
for running your kernels,
whether or not it's CPU or GPU,

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:25.560
a whole variety of
TensorFlow frameworks

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:27.464
with various notebooks.

00:17:27.464 --> 00:17:29.130
And then it also
allows you to do things

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:32.770
like set the CPUs, set the
memory, extra resources,

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:35.880
including things like GPUs,
which is obviously very, very

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:37.980
important.

00:17:37.980 --> 00:17:42.740
So with that, we're not going to
do interactive Jupiter training

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:43.400
today.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:45.410
We've already decided
to use TensorFlow,

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:46.527
and it's pretty good.

00:17:46.527 --> 00:17:48.860
So we're going to do what's
called distributed training.

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:50.300
That's where you
take that model,

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:51.800
that Tensor2Tensor model.

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:53.810
You package it in a
container, and then you're

00:17:53.810 --> 00:17:56.270
able-- you know, with a very
few amount of commands--

00:17:56.270 --> 00:17:58.850
and you're able to distribute
it across an entire cluster.

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:01.400
And that means
that it's up to you

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:05.300
to now decide how many
nodes you want to use

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:06.480
as part of your training.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:09.440
So if you want it to go faster,
you could add 10, 100, 1,000.

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:12.260
If you wanted to go slowly or
you're more cost conscious,

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:14.930
you could use just two or three.

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:16.700
So we'll go and we'll do that.

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:20.280
To run a distributed training,
you need to do one command--

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:21.630
or you need to do two things.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:24.740
The first thing you need to
do is set some parameters,

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:27.230
and this is really where
the power of Kubeflow

00:18:27.230 --> 00:18:30.180
comes from or
starts to highlight.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:33.080
First, you saw me set up
an entire framework earlier

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:36.410
using a very straightforward
command to select

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:37.760
the package that I want.

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:42.800
But now, I'm able to set
parameters for the specific job

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:46.010
that I'm going to run without
changing any of that underlying

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:46.550
code.

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:48.216
And these are parameters
you understand.

00:18:48.216 --> 00:18:50.360
You know, whether or not
there's a GPU present,

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:52.640
how many workers
there are, where

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.740
I'm storing my data, all
these various things.

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:58.210
You're able to do it just like
setting any other variable.

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:02.367
So I'm setting up for
a distributed training,

00:19:02.367 --> 00:19:02.950
and here I am.

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:06.790
I'm kicking off with this
same command you saw earlier.

00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:08.830
I'm kicking off
Tensor2Tensor, which

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:11.770
I have built into a container.

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:14.200
And just to prove that it
is, in fact, running here,

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:18.030
you can see that the
Tensor2Tensor job is running.

00:19:18.030 --> 00:19:19.780
It's running against
CPUs because I'm only

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:23.940
using my local laptop and
I want to have it run.

00:19:23.940 --> 00:19:27.950
And here, you can see you
get the exact same logs

00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:30.740
that you would run from
any normal TensorFlow

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.650
job with all the appropriate
information there.

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:35.720
Again, nothing special here.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:36.680
This is just--

00:19:36.680 --> 00:19:38.570
I wouldn't ask you to
read this wall of text.

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:40.400
I'm just pointing
out that this really

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:43.490
is a real live job running,
and you saw me execute just,

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:47.090
you know, four or five commands
to get it off the ground.

00:19:47.090 --> 00:19:47.990
All right.

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:53.180
So now that I've done
that, it's running OK.

00:19:53.180 --> 00:19:55.460
But what you saw is you
didn't see any syntax errors.

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:57.110
You know that it ran properly.

00:19:57.110 --> 00:19:58.940
Maybe it converged,
maybe it didn't.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:00.470
That's OK.

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:02.390
All I'm really doing
on my local laptop

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:04.400
is making sure that
this does run properly

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:06.510
without any errors.

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:08.490
What I'd like to do now
is, now that I said,

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:10.430
hey, this does run
great, I'd like

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:12.860
to take it to a
cluster in the cloud

00:20:12.860 --> 00:20:15.780
to run that training
very, very quickly.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:19.700
And so in this case, I've set
up a GKE, a Google Kubernetes

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:20.990
Engine cluster.

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:24.650
It runs on Google
Cloud, obviously,

00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:28.490
and it's going to run with
two different sets of nodes.

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:32.300
First, it's going to
run with GPU nodes.

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:37.010
And those are the absolute
latest from NVIDIA

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:41.240
with a whole variety of
different nodes and node pools.

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:45.590
You can see here that we're
using eight NVIDIA Tesla

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:48.920
p100 accelerators
attached to those nodes.

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:50.480
And then second,
we're also going

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:53.960
to use the brand new
TPUs from Google.

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:56.230
Let me just do that.

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:58.210
You can see the p100 there.

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:01.000
And second, we're
going to use the TPUs.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.310
Those are the tensor
processing units

00:21:03.310 --> 00:21:05.230
attached to the same cluster.

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:07.510
And we're going to run
those jobs simultaneously

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:10.600
across those clusters to
see which one runs first.

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:12.760
Sometimes, p100s run better.

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:14.320
Sometimes TPUs run better.

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:15.850
It doesn't matter
to us because we

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:18.160
have-- it's so easy to
set up multiple of these.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:21.460
We can run the
experiment ourselves,

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:24.580
and you can see here this
is what the TPUs look like.

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:26.560
And they support
TensorFlow version 1.7,

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.980
so we'll make sure to
support that in our nodes.

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:30.190
OK.

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:35.914
So now that we've kicked it
off, we're going to run this.

00:21:35.914 --> 00:21:37.830
And now, we're going to
switch to that cluster

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:39.390
that I just showed
you, and we're

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:43.570
going to apply that exact same
deployment that I ran locally.

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:46.950
And again, this is part
of the power of Kubeflow.

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:48.730
I don't have to
make big changes.

00:21:48.730 --> 00:21:51.000
In fact, in this case,
I'm making no changes.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:53.340
From the exact same
deployment I had locally,

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:55.740
I'm able to deploy
to my GKE cluster.

00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:57.660
That makes it easy
for me to get started,

00:21:57.660 --> 00:22:00.780
easy for me to have
reproduceable machine learning

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:03.140
and data science.

00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:05.450
And you can see there,
it's updating it

00:22:05.450 --> 00:22:08.660
in the cluster on the cloud.

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:13.260
Next, I'm going to kick
off my GPU training.

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:15.350
Again, I'm going to
use that same package

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:17.750
that I had built
earlier for running

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:20.570
on my local machine that
was running against CPUs.

00:22:20.570 --> 00:22:23.161
But this time, I'm going to
change the parameters and say,

00:22:23.161 --> 00:22:23.660
no.

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:26.480
In fact, I do have
GPUs on these nodes,

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.510
and I want you to pick it up.

00:22:29.510 --> 00:22:31.204
And then, I'm also
going to change here

00:22:31.204 --> 00:22:32.870
where the data is
stored, and again, I'm

00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:35.270
doing that based on parameters.

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:36.890
Not changing code.

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:39.740
And I apply those,
and those should

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:41.990
be kicked off momentarily.

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:45.510
Second, I'm also going
to deploy that to TPUs.

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:49.730
And so again, I'm going
to change a parameter

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:53.270
to make sure that this new
job is running against TPUs.

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:55.850
And I'm also going to use
a slightly different build.

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:59.186
Unfortunately today, you have
to build different versions

00:22:59.186 --> 00:23:01.310
based on whether or not
you're using GPUs and TPUs.

00:23:01.310 --> 00:23:04.790
Again, for data
scientists, this is great

00:23:04.790 --> 00:23:08.210
because it means you're able
to package and describe exactly

00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:10.660
how you want that model to run.

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:11.300
OK.

00:23:11.300 --> 00:23:13.380
So we're kicking
that off as well.

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:16.550
This time, I don't have
to go into my local logs

00:23:16.550 --> 00:23:19.250
to do it because Google
Kubernetes Engine actually

00:23:19.250 --> 00:23:22.136
has a great UI for me
to explore what jobs

00:23:22.136 --> 00:23:23.260
are running simultaneously.

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:27.140
So here, you can see all the
logs, the current loss rate--

00:23:27.140 --> 00:23:28.970
it's happening very quickly.

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:33.180
It's saving checkpoints into
that Google Storage bucket,

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:35.390
and you can see here all
the various jobs that

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:37.920
are running simultaneously.

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:38.900
OK.

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:40.820
So the jobs are kicked off.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:41.570
They're running.

00:23:41.570 --> 00:23:43.070
Again, we're not
going to bore you

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:45.080
with how long it takes
to run all these.

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:47.960
I'm going to use, in
fact, TensorBoard.

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:50.930
And in this case, TensorBoard
also ships with Kubeflow.

00:23:50.930 --> 00:23:54.350
It's built in, and it shows you
how quickly things are stepping

00:23:54.350 --> 00:23:56.810
through the overall
process and how quickly

00:23:56.810 --> 00:23:58.400
things are resolving.

00:23:58.400 --> 00:23:59.450
Great UI.

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:01.850
It helps your data
scientists really debug

00:24:01.850 --> 00:24:05.220
and understand what's
going on under the hood.

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:07.480
And most importantly,
it ships in the box.

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:09.980
You saw-- literally
exactly what you saw

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:11.990
earlier is what's running now.

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:14.990
I didn't have to do any special
new packaging or distributions

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:17.390
to run this, and yet, it
ran great on my laptop.

00:24:17.390 --> 00:24:19.240
It ran great on GPUs and TPUs.

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:22.200
In this case, we're able
to see, roughly speaking,

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:25.940
about 16 and 1/2 steps
per second for the TPUs,

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:29.480
and you saw earlier about three
steps per second for the GPUs.

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:31.700
So in one command,
I'm able to test

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:33.380
against multiple
different accelerators.

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:37.160
And in this case, the TPUs came
out about five times faster.

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:39.710
Really, really powerful stuff,
and we're really, really

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:41.870
excited to show you.

00:24:41.870 --> 00:24:44.960
And then finally, like I said,
the demo needs to be short.

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:48.590
So we'll try and jump
forward here a little bit.

00:24:48.590 --> 00:24:50.450
Also included in
the Kubeflow box,

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:52.940
I promise, you know,
it's not unlimited,

00:24:52.940 --> 00:24:54.695
but there is a lot
of stuff in there.

00:24:54.695 --> 00:24:56.510
It's TensorFlow Serving.

00:24:56.510 --> 00:25:00.170
So in this case, you
have TensorFlow Serving,

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:02.300
which means we're going
to take that trained model

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:04.591
and we're actually going to
will serve it and attach it

00:25:04.591 --> 00:25:06.710
to that Yelp review
we saw earlier.

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:12.440
We deploy that serving, and
now we're going to, excuse me,

00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:14.120
we're going to swap over.

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:18.170
You can see here in the
Google Kubernetes Engine UI

00:25:18.170 --> 00:25:23.000
that Tensor2Tensor
serving is running

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:25.770
and ready to receive results.

00:25:25.770 --> 00:25:28.310
So we'll go back
to our Yelp review.

00:25:28.310 --> 00:25:32.120
This is a different web page
that is now designed to use ML.

00:25:32.120 --> 00:25:33.860
Here were the original results.

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:34.614
All wrong.

00:25:34.614 --> 00:25:36.530
One that was happy when
it should've been sad,

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:38.529
one that was sad when it
should have been happy,

00:25:38.529 --> 00:25:40.190
and then the final
one that was happy

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:41.570
when it should have been sad.

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:44.930
And we're going to reattach
that, swap back over

00:25:44.930 --> 00:25:48.320
and go over to the UI, and hit
the predict sentiment again.

00:25:48.320 --> 00:25:51.560
And it now comes out
correct, and so that's really

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:53.690
the summary of Kubeflow.

00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:56.300
It's a very, very
straightforward framework

00:25:56.300 --> 00:25:59.579
that really empowers you to
make decisions about machine--

00:25:59.579 --> 00:26:01.370
the best machine learning
framework for you

00:26:01.370 --> 00:26:02.930
without having to worry
about any of the stuff

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:03.560
under the hood.

00:26:03.560 --> 00:26:05.870
JAY SMITH: I mean,
that's definitely--

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:08.540
I mean, it seems like it's leaps
and bounds for machine learning

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:12.480
because I can, like you
showed with Minikube,

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:15.270
run the cluster on my
machine and whether it

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:19.360
be servers in a cold load,
or servers in my office,

00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:22.200
or even a cloud, Google
Cloud, I can just

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:27.157
move it and continue running
my models with more power.

00:26:27.157 --> 00:26:28.740
And then, of course,
it comes packaged

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:31.054
with all those great little
tools, like TensorBoard.

00:26:31.054 --> 00:26:32.220
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:33.417
And that really is it.

00:26:33.417 --> 00:26:36.000
When you go and talk to almost
any data scientist in the world

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:40.924
today, they often have
very dispersed environments

00:26:40.924 --> 00:26:42.090
that they'll need to run in.

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:45.900
They want to be moved
to wherever the data is.

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:48.630
They may have petabytes of
data that they want to process,

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:51.870
and that may not be in
this cloud or that cloud.

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:55.200
And what they're really looking
to do is get to an environment

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:58.210
where they can think
purely about the model

00:26:58.210 --> 00:27:02.280
and let the separation in IT
ops, you know, take care of it

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:04.110
for them.

00:27:04.110 --> 00:27:07.090
What you didn't see there were
some really important points.

00:27:07.090 --> 00:27:09.570
First, you didn't see
any bespoke solutions.

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:13.140
These weren't custom,
new ML frameworks.

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:15.780
They weren't brand new
open source solutions.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:19.080
We're using existing
very popular open source

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:23.550
technologies today that you can
use or swap out for your own.

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:26.560
Things aren't, like, insanely
tightly wired together.

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:29.130
Second, you didn't see any
cloud-specific technology.

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:32.020
Even when we were deploying
to Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:27:32.020 --> 00:27:34.320
we were using the
exact same software

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:35.910
that we had running locally.

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:38.037
All we did was swap
out our parameters.

00:27:38.037 --> 00:27:40.620
And then finally, you didn't see
any forking of the Kubernetes

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:41.520
APIs.

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:44.820
Meaning, anywhere you have a
Kubernetes compliant cluster,

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:47.470
it's going to run great.

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:49.640
And really, this
is going to empower

00:27:49.640 --> 00:27:53.870
a brand new set
of data scientists

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:55.470
and other people in the world.

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:57.560
There's a great
story out of Sweden

00:27:57.560 --> 00:28:00.890
where a group of scientists
were able to look

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:04.910
at a number of data and identify
that what they were doing wrong

00:28:04.910 --> 00:28:08.480
was they were plowing the wrong
streets at the wrong time.

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:11.630
And stay at home
parents and women

00:28:11.630 --> 00:28:13.670
who happened to not
be at work that day

00:28:13.670 --> 00:28:16.460
ended up being injured
far more frequently

00:28:16.460 --> 00:28:18.680
because in light
snow situations,

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:21.830
they would be tripping and
falling on sidewalks when

00:28:21.830 --> 00:28:23.750
cars could actually handle it.

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:25.340
So by looking at
the data scientists

00:28:25.340 --> 00:28:27.470
and using frameworks
just like Kubeflow,

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:29.180
they were able to
see that they could

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:31.790
make a huge impact
in people's lives

00:28:31.790 --> 00:28:34.490
without any change
or any other budget.

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:37.310
Just by changing the
order, and that's really

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:40.520
what we need to do as
far as using Kubeflow.

00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:43.850
It's really empowering
everyone at the various levels

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:46.640
to make smart
decisions, and help them

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:48.800
make the world a better place.

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:53.210
And by doing that, by focusing
on giving people clean layers,

00:28:53.210 --> 00:28:54.950
by giving the data
scientists the ability

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:57.830
to make these decisions,
they're able to hand off

00:28:57.830 --> 00:29:00.650
to the next layer above them
to make even better decisions

00:29:00.650 --> 00:29:02.300
and really change the world.

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:04.250
We are just getting
started with Kubeflow.

00:29:04.250 --> 00:29:05.660
It is 0.1.

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:07.580
It's a little early for
running in production.

00:29:07.580 --> 00:29:10.580
Even though all the components
of Kubeflow are battle tested,

00:29:10.580 --> 00:29:12.650
things like TensorFlow,
as I mentioned,

00:29:12.650 --> 00:29:14.150
TensorBoard, Jupiter
and things like

00:29:14.150 --> 00:29:17.040
that, have been out
for many, many months,

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:19.520
Kubeflow is still brand new.

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:21.320
But we do have a lot
of people helping.

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:23.960
You can see a small subset of
people who are contributing

00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:25.710
to the platform today.

00:29:25.710 --> 00:29:29.630
And then what's next in Kubeflow
0.2, which we hope to have out

00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:32.060
by this summer, we're going
to make it even easier

00:29:32.060 --> 00:29:36.140
to set it up, integrate with
more and deeper Kubernetes

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:39.200
features, and a lot
better packages.

00:29:39.200 --> 00:29:41.390
But really, the feature
we'd like to have

00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:42.740
is the one we haven't heard yet.

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:44.600
There are many, many
data scientists out there

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:46.141
that are trying to
solve the problem,

00:29:46.141 --> 00:29:49.430
and Kubeflow is entirely
an open source project.

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:53.000
It is, though Google
contributed to it significantly,

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:56.000
we have over 70 contributors
from 17 different companies

00:29:56.000 --> 00:29:57.230
working on it today.

00:29:57.230 --> 00:29:59.780
And we want to make sure it
truly does run everywhere,

00:29:59.780 --> 00:30:02.900
and so if you find that there
is a machine learning problem

00:30:02.900 --> 00:30:06.800
that you're running into today,
we would love to hear about it.

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:08.850
JAY SMITH: We're on GitHub.

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:10.700
DAVID ARONCHICK: Exactly.

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:12.800
And so with that, that's a wrap.

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:15.440
You know, like I said, we have
all the various places for us

00:30:15.440 --> 00:30:16.981
to reach out, and
I think we're going

00:30:16.981 --> 00:30:19.205
to take just a moment to
collect the questions.

00:31:07.845 --> 00:31:09.220
JAY SMITH: All
right, we're back.

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:12.270
So let's go into
some questions here.

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:16.680
What does the adoption of
Kubeflow look like in the wild?

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:19.290
DAVID ARONCHICK: So the
reality is, I wasn't joking.

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:21.860
Kubeflow is a 0.1
release right now.

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:26.000
We would actually not recommend
using it in too many production

00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:28.010
workloads right now.

00:31:28.010 --> 00:31:30.620
That said, we have heard
many, many stories,

00:31:30.620 --> 00:31:34.130
and you can go and check out
the Kubeflow.org website coming

00:31:34.130 --> 00:31:34.720
soon.

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:36.250
Not quite available yet.

00:31:36.250 --> 00:31:38.840
Or our GitHub repo to
see a number of places

00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:40.730
that people are using it today.

00:31:40.730 --> 00:31:43.370
In the examples directory,
you will already

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:47.270
see a great number
of examples there,

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:50.450
a solution around GitHub
summarization, GitHub issue

00:31:50.450 --> 00:31:53.480
summarization, the tensor
is obviously built in,

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.190
and a number of
other ones where you

00:31:55.190 --> 00:31:56.690
can download and
start experimenting

00:31:56.690 --> 00:31:57.880
with Kubeflow today.

00:31:57.880 --> 00:31:59.210
JAY SMITH: Awesome.

00:31:59.210 --> 00:32:01.164
So does Kubeflow run on GKE?

00:32:01.164 --> 00:32:02.330
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:06.410
So the demo that you saw
there was Kubeflow running

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:08.510
on Google Kubernetes engine.

00:32:08.510 --> 00:32:10.340
And it obviously
runs great, and it

00:32:10.340 --> 00:32:12.230
takes advantage of
all the elements

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:15.700
that are available in Google
Cloud, node pools, preemptible

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:19.670
VMs, GPUs, TPUs,
so on and so forth.

00:32:19.670 --> 00:32:22.910
But like I said, it really
does run anywhere on Google,

00:32:22.910 --> 00:32:24.470
excuse me, Kubernetes runs.

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:27.050
And so that might be
another cloud or on-prem.

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:29.660
But obviously, we think that
we can do a pretty good job

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:32.960
serving Kubeflow on GKE.

00:32:32.960 --> 00:32:33.870
JAY SMITH: Of course.

00:32:33.870 --> 00:32:35.680
Oh, here's a very
interesting one.

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:40.714
When would I use Kubeflow
over Cloud ML Engine?

00:32:40.714 --> 00:32:41.880
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:43.338
So this is a question
we get a lot,

00:32:43.338 --> 00:32:45.900
and the reality is, is
a cloud machine learning

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:47.080
engine is fantastic.

00:32:47.080 --> 00:32:48.870
We strongly recommend it.

00:32:48.870 --> 00:32:52.350
What we have found, though,
is that oftentimes, a data

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:55.110
scientist might want to
swap out or need something

00:32:55.110 --> 00:32:57.210
in a hybrid or on-prem solution.

00:32:57.210 --> 00:32:59.730
And in those cases, that's when
you would want to swap out.

00:32:59.730 --> 00:33:02.646
So basically, the summary is,
if you'd like a hosted solution

00:33:02.646 --> 00:33:04.770
where you really don't have
to think about anything

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.230
under the hood, you just want
to hand a model to something

00:33:07.230 --> 00:33:09.980
and get back an answer
or use it for inference.

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:11.730
Cloud machine
learning is fantastic,

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:13.350
and we would strongly
recommend it.

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:16.920
If you have customization, or
you want to do on premises,

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:18.987
or you want to do
a hybrid scenario,

00:33:18.987 --> 00:33:20.320
Kubeflow would be your best bet.

00:33:20.320 --> 00:33:22.842
They really do layer over
each other very nicely.

00:33:22.842 --> 00:33:23.550
JAY SMITH: Right.

00:33:23.550 --> 00:33:26.140
So right tool for the
right job kind of thing.

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:27.640
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:33:27.640 --> 00:33:31.540
JAY SMITH: If I am an ML, data
scientist, how will Kubeflow

00:33:31.540 --> 00:33:34.150
simplify my daily life
rather than complicate it

00:33:34.150 --> 00:33:36.250
by adding the Kubernetes layer?

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:37.579
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:33:37.579 --> 00:33:39.370
You know, I think one
of the biggest things

00:33:39.370 --> 00:33:41.650
here is that a lot
of data scientists

00:33:41.650 --> 00:33:44.230
really want to operate
at that next level up.

00:33:44.230 --> 00:33:46.355
I mean, I can't tell you
how many data scientists--

00:33:46.355 --> 00:33:49.420
I was just talking with a
fantastic researcher last week

00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:53.950
who literally couldn't reproduce
their experiment from a year

00:33:53.950 --> 00:33:56.681
ago because the versions
of Python had changed,

00:33:56.681 --> 00:33:58.180
and they weren't
sure what happened,

00:33:58.180 --> 00:34:01.360
and their library
dependencies had failed.

00:34:01.360 --> 00:34:05.170
Kubeflow allows you to
describe your entire framework

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:08.199
in a containerized,
hermetically sealed, dropped

00:34:08.199 --> 00:34:12.803
in amber kind of scenario,
and really detail it forever.

00:34:12.803 --> 00:34:15.469
And so that's something that you
can share with your colleagues.

00:34:15.469 --> 00:34:16.927
You can put it in
a research paper,

00:34:16.927 --> 00:34:18.210
all those various things.

00:34:18.210 --> 00:34:20.980
And it means you also don't
have to solve all the problems

00:34:20.980 --> 00:34:21.850
under the hood.

00:34:21.850 --> 00:34:23.909
You don't have to deal
with service discovery.

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:26.440
You don't have to deal with
provisioning persistent disk.

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:29.320
You don't have to deal
with attaching drivers

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:31.080
and GPUs and things like that.

00:34:31.080 --> 00:34:33.580
Kubernetes takes care of
all that stuff for you.

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:35.739
Now, that said, if you
are just experimenting

00:34:35.739 --> 00:34:38.920
and you want to use a single, we
call it kitchen sink container,

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:40.610
that can be perfectly fine.

00:34:40.610 --> 00:34:42.520
What we found,
though, is more often

00:34:42.520 --> 00:34:46.000
than not what happens is
that, immediately following

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:47.920
that experiment, a
data scientist has

00:34:47.920 --> 00:34:50.770
to completely rewrite
their entire model,

00:34:50.770 --> 00:34:52.570
completely rewrite all
their dependencies,

00:34:52.570 --> 00:34:54.760
and set up, and have
a lot of trouble even

00:34:54.760 --> 00:34:57.400
reproducing the experiments they
were doing in the first place

00:34:57.400 --> 00:35:00.520
because that experiment that
they were running initially

00:35:00.520 --> 00:35:01.960
is no longer production ready.

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:04.480
And that's where we hope
to make a lot of distance

00:35:04.480 --> 00:35:05.680
with Kubeflow.

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:07.750
Kubernetes is very,
very easy to set up.

00:35:07.750 --> 00:35:10.800
It comes in Docker,
it has a mini cube.

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:12.790
It's set up, and
then you can deploy--

00:35:12.790 --> 00:35:16.300
you can describe and deploy your
entire framework of Kubeflow

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:18.460
onto that local
cluster, and that

00:35:18.460 --> 00:35:19.990
is a production ready solution.

00:35:19.990 --> 00:35:21.727
That's something you
can take elsewhere.

00:35:21.727 --> 00:35:22.810
JAY SMITH: Very good, yes.

00:35:22.810 --> 00:35:24.550
Kubernetes, there
is a little bit

00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:27.940
of a learning curve with it,
but it's pretty easy to set up.

00:35:27.940 --> 00:35:29.980
And again, it's much
easier, as you mentioned,

00:35:29.980 --> 00:35:36.700
to control versus learning how
to manage YUM or app repository

00:35:36.700 --> 00:35:38.934
and get all the right
dependencies and everything.

00:35:38.934 --> 00:35:39.850
DAVID ARONCHICK: Yeah.

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:43.390
I mean, the demo you
saw today was totally,

00:35:43.390 --> 00:35:46.090
you know, live, real demo.

00:35:46.090 --> 00:35:49.000
And you saw no
Kubernetes commands.

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:53.140
Literally none, other
than just to show off,

00:35:53.140 --> 00:35:55.330
you know, that something
was actually running.

00:35:55.330 --> 00:35:57.490
All the systems were
running and trying

00:35:57.490 --> 00:36:00.640
your model were built in and
out that data science layer

00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:02.050
that you're all familiar with.

00:36:02.050 --> 00:36:02.925
JAY SMITH: All right.

00:36:02.925 --> 00:36:04.660
Looks like we got one
more question here.

00:36:04.660 --> 00:36:06.704
What about data in
model versioning?

00:36:06.704 --> 00:36:07.870
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely.

00:36:07.870 --> 00:36:09.700
That's something
that's very actively

00:36:09.700 --> 00:36:13.630
being discussed in the
Kubeflow community right now.

00:36:13.630 --> 00:36:15.790
We have heard this
request a lot.

00:36:15.790 --> 00:36:18.370
This is something where we
really do want to empower

00:36:18.370 --> 00:36:21.250
the community to make these
decisions and help think about

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:23.940
what should come in
the box and what isn't.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:27.350
Kubeflow isn't trying to
solve a lot of these problems.

00:36:27.350 --> 00:36:29.620
We're really packaging
the best of breed

00:36:29.620 --> 00:36:30.920
that's out there today.

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:33.190
And so in many of
those discussions that

00:36:33.190 --> 00:36:35.410
are happening right now in
the cloud or, excuse me,

00:36:35.410 --> 00:36:39.550
in the community,
you are seeing folks

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:44.980
begin to come up with some
norms about what data and model

00:36:44.980 --> 00:36:46.600
versioning should look like.

00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:48.760
And when those
norms land, we will

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:51.010
ask people who
want to participate

00:36:51.010 --> 00:36:53.590
in those discussions
to snap to those norms.

00:36:53.590 --> 00:36:55.555
So it'd basically
say, hey, if you're

00:36:55.555 --> 00:36:57.430
going to run a machine
learning or excuse me,

00:36:57.430 --> 00:37:00.550
a model version or data
versioning pipeline,

00:37:00.550 --> 00:37:02.154
you should do one,
two, three, four.

00:37:02.154 --> 00:37:04.570
And as long as any solution
comes along and does one, two,

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:06.920
three, four, it should run fine.

00:37:06.920 --> 00:37:08.170
JAY SMITH: Well, that's great.

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:11.840
Well, thank you David so much
for joining us and telling

00:37:11.840 --> 00:37:13.940
everybody about the
wonders of Kubeflow.

00:37:13.940 --> 00:37:16.130
Hopefully, we can get some
people to start using it

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:17.630
and start contributing.

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:19.640
And we want to
see all those pull

00:37:19.640 --> 00:37:23.860
requests coming into GitHub and
also hear your success story.

00:37:23.860 --> 00:37:26.472
So it's definitely
been great to have you.

00:37:26.472 --> 00:37:28.430
DAVID ARONCHICK: Absolutely,
thank you so much.

00:37:28.430 --> 00:37:30.520
JAY SMITH: Well, thank you
very much for watching,

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:34.760
